	"helpwlaninfo1":"Turn on wireless: If you want to make wireless work, you must select this checkbox.",
	"helpwlaninfo2":"Hide access points: If you want to hide access points for your router, you must choose this box. In this case, the configuration cannot obtain the SSID through passive scanning.",
	"helpwlaninfo3":"SSID: SSID (Service Set Identification) is a unique name shared in a wireless network. The SSID must be the same for all devices on the network.",
	"helpwlaninfo4":"Country: The channel will adjust the frequency according to different countries to adapt to the frequency regulations of different countries.",
	"helpnetremoteinfo1":"Remote management allows you to make configuration settings from the WAN (Wide Area Network) via a web browser. Access to the browser interface still requires a username and password to log in.",
	"helpnetremoteinfo2":"IP Address: The Internet IP address of the access router. If the specified IP address is 0.0.0.0, all hosts can be connected to DI-624+A for configuration settings.",
	"helpnetremoteinfo3":"Port: Access the port number of the router. For example: http://x.x.x.x:8080, x.x.x.x is the WAN IP address of the router, and 8080 is the port of the Web-management interface.",
	"helpqosinfo":"QoS is a security mechanism of the network. It is a technology used to solve problems such as network delay and congestion. QoS refers to the performance of packet transmission throughput, delay, delay jitter, and loss rate.",
	"helpnettimeinfo":"Check 'Automatically synchronize with Internet time server' and the terminal will synchronize with the Internet time.",
	"staticrouting":"Static route",
	"helpnetroute1":"Destination IP: The IP address to which the packet arrived.",
	"helpnetroute2":"Mask: The subnet mask of the destination IP.",
	"helpnetroute3":"Gateway: The gateway through which the packet will pass.",
	"helpnetroute4":"Interface: The interface selected when the packet passes through the terminal.",
	"helpsafewaninfo1":"Destination IP: The IP address to which the packet arrived.",
	"helpsafewaninfo2":"Use the URL to deny the LAN side calculator access to a specific web side. A URL is a string that is specifically orchestrated for a regional network. If any of the URLs are included in the blocking word, they will not be accessible. If any of the URLs are included in the blockade, the page will not be displayed.",
	"helpsafefirewallinfo":"The firewall is used to allow or deny packet data through the machine. It works in the same way as IP filters.",
	"helpsafemacinfo":"MAC (Media Access Control) Address filtering allows the user's calculator's MAC address to allow or deny access to the LAN and the Internet. Use MAC Filter to filter users connected to LAN ports and wireless.",
	"helpsafeipportinfo1":"Use IP address filters to deny specific IP addresses access to data on the Internet. You can reject specific ports or all ports of a specific IP address. The defined port will be displayed on the screen. To use them, you can choose to edit the project. You only need to enter the calculator LAN IP address to define access to the Internet.",
	"helpsafeipportinfo2":"IP: The IP address is the data that accesses the Internet on the LAN side calculator. You can add an IP range or an IP address.",
	"helpsafeipportinfo3":"Port: Defines Internet access using a single port or a range of ports. If no specific port is defined, all ports will be denied access.",
	"helpsafeipportinfo4":"Click 'Security' --> 'IP Port Filtering'. By default, the firewall is enabled. Fire prevention is used to prevent the transfer of files between the Internet and PCs. Only authenticated files can be transferred to the LAN side.",
	"helpuseddnsinfo1":"Allow dynamic hostname alias dynamic IP addresses for any domain. Allows routers to access the Internet more easily from different regions.",
	"helpuseddnsinfo2":"If you have a dynamic DNS account, you can set this feature on your router.",
	"helpuseddnsinfo3":"Provider: Select DDNS to use the server in the table below.",
	"helpuseddnsinfo4":"Host Name: Please enter the host name of your DDNS account.",
	"helpuseddnsinfo5":"Username/E-mail: Please enter the username of your DDNS account.",
	"helpuseddnsinfo6":"Password / Key: Please enter the password for your DDNS account.",
	"natdmz":"NAT -- DMZ host",
	"helpusernatdmzinfo1":"Disarming the DMZ is a technique that allows the terminal to allow all incoming packets to be specified inside the packet. Making the LAN on the Internet can make a PC completely exposed to any user, and it can also allow a specified IP address and other Internet users or servers to communicate with each other without restrictions, allowing more applications to run on the specified IP. address. Specify an IP address to receive all distinguishable connections and files. On the DMZ Host IP Address page, click 'Save/Apply' to activate the DMZ host.",
	"helpusernatdmzinfo2":"If you have a calculator that does not properly operate an Internet application under a router, you can grant this calculator unlimited access to the Internet. The calculator set under the DMZ (Demilitarized Zone) has unlimited access to the Internet. Setting the client calculator to the DMZ will put the calculator at various security risks, so using this option is the most compelling method.",
